 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "LOCH KATRINE. Since 1859 this loch has supplied Glasgow with its drinking water, piped some twenty-six miles to the city, and it was opened by Queen Victoria. A steamship, the Sir Walter Scott, has carried visitors on the loch since 1900, and is still sailing. Glasgow has never forgotten who to thank.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_katrine",
   "title": "Loch Katrine"
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "ROB ROY MACGREGOR. Born in 1671, a cattle dealer turned outlaw and folk hero who spent his last years at Balquhidder. He is buried in the kirkyard here, and the grave is the thing visitors come to see. Walter Scott gave him a novel in 1817 and the legend never looked back.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_rob_roy",
   "title": "Rob Roy at Balquhidder"
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "DOUNE CASTLE. A fourteenth-century courtyard castle built for Robert Stewart, Duke of Albany. It has played a castle in a great many films, and a very silly one about a grail. It has never once complained about the extras."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE ROB ROY WAY. About eighty miles from Drymen, on the edge of the Trossachs, to Pitlochry, through glens, lochs and places that have had a bad deal from the weather and a good one from Walter Scott."
  }
 ],
